{"kind":"project","schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"variants":[{"paths":["\/tutorials\/adaengine\/eventlisteners"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"metadata":{"categoryPathComponent":"AdaEngine","category":"Ada","title":"Event Listeners in Ada","role":"project"},"sections":[{"chapter":"Create your first 2D game project.","xcodeRequirement":"Xcode 26.2 or later","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In this tutorial you will learn about the event listeners system in Ada."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"How can you catch events and process them"}]}],"estimatedTimeInMinutes":5,"kind":"hero","title":"Event Listeners in Ada"},{"kind":"tasks","tasks":[{"title":"Base of Event-Subscriber pattern","anchor":"Base-of-Event-Subscriber-pattern","contentSection":[{"mediaPosition":"trailing","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Event listeners in Ada are built on the Observer pattern."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Its main concept is based on the fact that there is a certain Subject that can send any events."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"And there is an Observer that can receive and process this event."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"In addition, Observer “subscribes” to events, as if you had subscribed to your favorite blog or magazine - as soon as an event occurs, Observer immediately knows about it.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"media":"EL-1.jpg","kind":"contentAndMedia"}],"stepsSection":[]},{"contentSection":[{"mediaPosition":"trailing","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Having understood how the Observer pattern works, let’s look at how to subscribe to scene events using the Plugin pattern.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"contentAndMedia"}],"stepsSection":[{"media":null,"code":"EL-2-1-scene.swift","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Let’s say we have a plugin in which we would like to listen to some events.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"caption":[],"type":"step","runtimePreview":null},{"runtimePreview":null,"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"First of all, to control subscription\/unsubscription to events and for proper memory management, we must create a "},{"code":"disposeBag","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" using the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Local"},{"text":" property wrapper.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"We also mark our plugin with ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@MainActor"},{"type":"text","text":" to ensure thread safety."}]}],"caption":[],"code":"EL-2-2-scene.swift","type":"step","media":null},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"After that, in the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"setup(in:)"},{"type":"text","text":" method you can call some code to subscribe to the event."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"app.main.subscribe"},{"type":"text","text":" method helps with this."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"When an event is triggered, our listener subscribes to it and in the callback we can process our event."}]}],"runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","caption":[],"media":null,"code":"EL-2-3-scene.swift"}],"title":"Subscribe to scene events","anchor":"Subscribe-to-scene-events"},{"anchor":"Type-of-events","stepsSection":[{"caption":[],"code":"EL-2-3-scene.swift","runtimePreview":null,"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"In the previous section, we saw the ","type":"text"},{"code":"SceneEvents.OnReady","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" event.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"Ada has several available events that you can handle."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"For example, you can find out what state your current game scene is in.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"You can add your own additional logic when any built-in event occurs.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"media":null,"type":"step"},{"media":null,"code":"EL-2-4-events.swift","runtimePreview":null,"caption":[],"type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Here, as an example, are some built-in events that may be useful to you."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Familiar scene events and new collision events."}]}]}],"contentSection":[{"kind":"contentAndMedia","mediaPosition":"trailing","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"What events can we use? Let’s look at a couple of code examples in Ada.","type":"text"}]}]}],"title":"Type of events"},{"title":"Additional for physics","stepsSection":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Let’s continue with the example we looked at earlier."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"We can access our event and assign the necessary parameters to the properties of the physical component.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Note that we use ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task { @MainActor in ... }"},{"text":" to ensure we’re on the main actor when modifying physics properties.","type":"text"}]}],"type":"step","media":null,"caption":[],"code":"EL-2-5-physics.swift","runtimePreview":null}],"contentSection":[{"kind":"contentAndMedia","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Additional information for handling physics in a game engine."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"With the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"physicsWorld2D"},{"type":"text","text":" component you can control the physics settings for any objects in our game."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"For example, you can set the gravity to zero for the scene creation event."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"mediaPosition":"trailing"}],"anchor":"Additional-for-physics"}]},{"action":{"overridingTitleInlineContent":[{"text":"Get started","type":"text"}],"id